.TH FIND 1 "October 8, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
find \- find files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B find
.I pathname
.RI [ expression ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Find
recursively descends the directory hierarchy from pathname seeking files
that match a boolean expression written in the primaries given below.
In numeric tests, +n means more than n, -n less than n, and n exactly n.
.TP
.B \-name pattern
Match the entry's name against a shell pattern.
.TP
.B \-path pattern
Match the whole walked pathname against a shell pattern.
.TP
.B \-type f|d
Select ordinary files or directories.
.TP
.B \-newer file
Compare modification timestamps with the named reference file.
.TP
.B \-mtime [+-]n
Compare modification age in calendar days, ignoring time of day.
Without a usable clock this test is false.
.TP
.B \-size [+-]n[c]
Compare file length in rounded-up 512-byte units; append c for bytes.
.TP
.B \-links [+-]n
Compare a link count of 1: ProDOS has no hard links.
.TP
.B \-xdev
Accepted; the traversal cannot cross a ProDOS volume boundary.
.TP
.B \-nouser, \-nogroup
Accepted as constant true: there are no account or group databases.
.TP
.B \-print
Print the current pathname; true.
.TP
.B \-prune
Do not descend into this directory; true.
.TP
.B \-exec command ... ;
True if the executed command returns exit status zero.
The end of the command is punctuated by a quoted semicolon.
A command argument '{}' is replaced by the current pathname.
.TP
.B \-ok command ... ;
Like -exec, but first write
.B < command ... pathname > ?
to the standard error and read one reply line from the terminal (the
standard error, which stays the console when the standard input is a
file). The command runs only if the reply begins with y or Y; otherwise
the primary is false and nothing runs. Where standard error is not a
terminal every reply is taken as no.
.TP
.B \-depth
Always true. Act on the entries of a directory before the directory
itself, so a tree is listed deepest first. -prune has no effect
when -depth is given.
.PP
The operators, in decreasing precedence, are ! (not), -a (and,
also implied by adjacency), and -o (or).
And/or short-circuit; parentheses group expressions.
Quote parentheses, pattern metacharacters and the semicolon for the shell:
.PP
.B find . \-name '*.S' \-exec echo '{}' ';'
.PP
If there is no -print, -exec or -ok, a -print is implicitly ANDed with
the complete expression.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/FIND  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sh(1), ls(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on a successful walk, 1 for access/path/spawn failures, 2 for syntax, complexity or traversal-depth errors. A child's nonzero -exec status makes that primary false, not itself an execution failure.
.SH BUGS
One starting path is accepted. The walk is limited to eight directory frames and 64-character full paths; at most three -exec primaries are allowed. No -perm, -user or -group: ProDOS files have no permission bits, owner or group to test, so the primaries are refused (exit status 2) rather than invented.
